            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for a part of a whole, then the Mandarin for all of it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: dàotián",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dàotián — \"rice paddy; paddy field\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "wǒ yéye nǎinai zhù zài dàotián pángbiān — My grandparents live next to the rice paddies."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "dàotián is built from dào 'rice in the field' and tián 'a field', both inherited from Middle Chinese, dawX and den (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the first as rice and the second, after an older book, as land that has been ploughed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for getting around."
